---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CornerFrame from '../components/ornaments/CornerFrame.astro';

const languages = await getCollection('languages');
const terms = await getCollection('terms');
const knowledge = await getCollection('knowledge');
const languageCount = languages.filter((l) => !l.data.isMoc).length;
const termCount = terms.filter((t) => !t.data.isMoc).length;
const knowledgeCount = knowledge.filter((k) => !k.data.isMoc).length;
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="What Learnitas is and how it's built.">
  <div class="about-page">
    <CornerFrame class="page-leaf">
      <div class="page-leaf-inner">
        <header class="about-header">
          <h1>About Learnitas</h1>
        </header>

        <div class="vault-prose drop-cap">
          <p>
            Learnitas is one person's ongoing notes on programming languages, computer science, and
            a growing set of other subjects, published as a browsable archive. Every page here,
            all {languageCount + termCount + knowledgeCount} of them, started as a plain markdown
            file in a private Obsidian vault, then was copied into this project's own{' '}
            <code>content/</code> folder and committed to source control. Editing happens in that
            private vault; publishing an update means syncing the change in and pushing it. The
            live site never reads the private vault directly, and never has write access back
            into it.
          </p>

          <h2>How it's built</h2>
          <p>
            The site is Astro, statically generated, with no server and no database. A Content
            Layer loader walks several folders under <code>content/</code> — "Programming
            Languages", "Terms and Knowledge" (computer science only), and one top-level folder
            per subject outside CS (Founders and Executives, Finance and Economics, Physics,
            Chemistry, Electrical and Electronics Engineering, each its own standalone section
            rather than a Terms & Knowledge sub-domain) — parses each note's frontmatter, and
            rewrites every <code>[[wikilink]]</code> into a real route by matching titles across
            the whole archive. Search is <a href="https://pagefind.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pagefind</a>,
            indexed after the production build — the header's dropdown shows a quick preview, and{' '}
            <a href="/search">the full search page</a> has everything else. Both only work once
            the site has been built for production, not in a plain dev server.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <a href="/graph">knowledge graph</a> and the "referenced by" list at the foot of
            every note are the same underlying data, just read in opposite directions: one is a
            note's outgoing links, the other is everyone else's incoming links to it. Nothing is
            computed twice.
          </p>

          <h2>Review</h2>
          <p>
            The <a href="/review">flashcard deck</a> is a small SM-2 flavored spaced-repetition
            scheduler, not a full SuperMemo implementation, running entirely in your browser's
            local storage. Cards are drawn from the "Definition:" line every note opens with. No
            question was hand-authored separately from the notes themselves; sharpen a note's
            definition and its card sharpens with it. The header's review icon shows a small
            count when cards are actually due, so the queue isn't only visible on the review page
            itself.
          </p>

          <h2>Reading it from outside a browser</h2>
          <p>
            Every note here is also available as plain JSON — no key, no account — at{' '}
            <a href="/api">/api</a>. It exists mainly for scripts and AI agents that want the
            vault's actual markdown rather than scraped HTML, but it's just as usable from a
            terminal with <code>curl</code>.
          </p>

          <h2>What's in it, and what's coming</h2>
          <p>
            Right now the archive holds {languageCount} programming language notes, {termCount}{' '}
            computer science term notes across the domains listed on the
            <a href="/terms">Terms &amp; Knowledge</a> page, and {knowledgeCount} notes across
            the standalone subjects in the sidebar below it — founders and startups, finance and
            economics, physics, chemistry, and electrical engineering, with more subjects likely
            to join them over time. All three numbers grow as new notes get synced in and
            committed — this page reads them the same way every other page does, live from the
            content folder at build time, so they're never manually updated by hand. Everything
            here is one person's understanding, written in the open and corrected as it turns out
            to be wrong, not a finished or authoritative reference —{' '}
            <a href="/rss.xml">subscribe via RSS</a> to follow what gets added, and if something's
            incorrect, every note has a link at the bottom to report it directly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </CornerFrame>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-page {
    max-width: 52rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2.5rem 2rem 6rem;
  }
  .page-leaf-inner {
    padding: 3.5rem 2.5rem;
  }
  .about-header {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0 auto 1.75rem;
  }
  .about-header h1 {
    font-size: 2.15rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .about-page {
      padding: 1.25rem 1rem 3rem;
    }
    .page-leaf-inner {
      padding: 1.75rem 1.35rem;
    }
    .about-header h1 {
      font-size: 1.6rem;
    }
  }
</style>
